n really try to mimic  um  their normal life um both with with  sticking to somewhat of a of a typical  routine  and finding ways even if you can't go  outside and and and have playdates with  with friends face to face trying to  encourage trying to schedule virtual  play dates um  making sure that you're scheduling time  for outdoor activity if you're in a  location where you're allowed to go  outside but certainly physical activity  we know has mood and mood boosting  effects um and and learning boosting  effects so i think uh certainly  scheduling in time for for physical  exercise and outdoor time um is really  critical both to support learning and  just and to support  mood  and again really trying to keep the day  is as close to a typical day as you can  i've had  i heard recently about a father who has  has a student with uh moderate  who's on the autism spectrum and and  drives  takes his son for a drive every morning  as though he were driving him to school  it's just a way to maintain some  semblance of of uh normalcy for him and  it really helps him to get into the  mindset of being in school so that may  not apply to the case of the student  with depression but really trying to  mimic things that that um that a typical  day uh  is helpful for students  and what what advice would you have we  have a quote i'll go ahead  oh i was just gonna say i will say one  other point for kids who are struggling  with social anxiety depression some of  the things that dr phillips and i are  talking about are not necessarily  intuitive and the great thing about at  least how organizations are adapting to  the coving crisis now is that  organizations like ours the child mind  institute and others have really moved  to a telehealth uh kind of forum where  not only are we working with active  patients but we'll also do brief  consultations on issues like this just  to ensure that families have support  during this time because you know this  is not intuitive how to deal wi